Strip Windows drive prefixes and backslash ".." paths from member names.
skip_unsafe_prefix() knew only "/" as a separator and nothing of drive
letters, so on Windows tar extracted members named C:/x, C:x, \x,
\\server\share\x, ..\x or a\..\..\x outside the current directory, and
"../C:/x" lost its "../" only to become absolute. Treat backslash as a
separator too, and strip a leading drive prefix wherever a leading
separator or "../" may be stripped. Also return "" for a name that is
".." after stripping, such as "/..", "../.." or "C:..", rather than
"..", as is already done for a bare "..". The same function names the
members of a new archive, so tar -c now stores c:/dir/x as dir/x.

